==========================================================================
DOMAIN CAVEAT, tested as a strict BICONDITIONAL
==========================================================================
  (D1) rule (*)  :   4494 out-of-domain references, ALL with coefficient exactly 0
                    31465 in-domain references,     NONE with coefficient 0
  (D2) rule (**) :   4494 out-of-domain references, ALL with coefficient exactly 0
                    31465 in-domain references,     NONE with coefficient 0
  n=2..29, all r, all b, every valid state
  VIOLATIONS: 0

  Note the ASYMMETRY, which is the trap:
    in (*)  the out-of-domain reference is m-1 = b+r,   one below g_r(.,b)'s min b+r+1
    in (**) the out-of-domain reference is n-a = b+r+1, one below g_r(.,b+1)'s min b+r+2
  Both are 'one below the minimum', but for DIFFERENT b, which is why a naive
  implementation that guards only (*) still diverges on (**).

==========================================================================
(D3) Theorem B at a=0 evaluates ghat_r(n+1, b+1) -- outside the
     probabilistic domain.  Is that a defect?
==========================================================================
  h_r(0,b) [true probability, raw recursion] == (n+1)/n * ghat_r(n+1, b+1)
    556 exact checks, 0 mismatches
  -> NOT a defect: ghat is a POLYNOMIAL expression, the Sec.4.1 identity is a
     polynomial identity in m valid at every integer, and the raw recursion
     itself never evaluates g_r out of domain (asserted throughout ref_sim.py).

